WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.570
How's this for a baptism by fire?

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.320
Just three days after being sworn into Congress

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.820
to represent Michigan's third district,

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.000
Republican freshmen, Peter Meijer, found himself

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.560
and his colleagues trapped without security

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.770
in the bowels of the Capitol building while a riot

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.340
that ultimately claimed five lives raged all around.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.240
The following week he was one of just 10 Republicans

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.180
and the only first-term member of the GOP

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.280
who voted to impeach Donald Trump,

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.170
whom he had supported on the campaign trail.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.470
Meijer's vote to impeach led to a narrowly failed censure vote

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.850
from his own state's GOP and immediate announcements

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.680
that he will be primaried in 2022.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.060
The 33-year-old Iraq War veteran,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.580
who says his military training helps him maintain focus,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.160
is filling the seat vacated

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.340
by former Libertarian Party Congressman Justin Amash,

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.990
whose policy views he shares on a broad range of issues.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.270
Meijer told Reason why he believes

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.560
in limited government, economic freedom, and individualism.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.260
Why he's against out of control stimulus spending

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.060
and military adventurism, and how he plans to combat

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.600
the craziness he sees both on the right

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.350
and the left in the House of Representatives.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.570
Representative Peter Meijer, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.553
Thank you for having me on Nick, a pleasure.

00:01:20.553 --> 00:01:25.210
Let's start with, you are one of just 10 Republicans

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.970
in the House of Representatives

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.230
who voted to impeach Trump the second time,

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.150
and from what I've read

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.140
you're the only first term-er to do that.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.760
Why did you vote to impeach Donald Trump,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.370
your party's president?

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.900
Oh well, you know, that question is something

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.160
that I know a lot of my constituents have been asking.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.640
I have had a number of town halls

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.580
and have heard a number of very impassioned opinions.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.700
You know to me it was a pretty simple question of,

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.930
is the Republican Party, a party of rule of law?

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.230
A party that believes in accountability?

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:03.070
Or is it one that puts a personality, you know,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:08.190
above principles?

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.200
The House's role in an impeachment is pretty narrow.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.090
We look at the article, or articles in this case,

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.710
there was only one of impeachment that are laid

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.250
before us and have to make a judgment

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.620
if we believe it to be true and accurate.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:21.920
And then if so,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.920
if impeachment is a reasonable decision to make obviously,

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:32.380
this was one that was politically less than optimal.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.680
I did not want in my first 10 days in office

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.230
to impeach a president of my own party,

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.940
but the fact of the matter is January 6th, and the assault

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.360
on the Capitol, would have never happened if it wasn't

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.350
for President Trump

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.610
propagating two lies.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.530
One, that November 3rd was a landslide victory

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.570
for Donald Trump that was stolen.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.900
And two, that January 6th was the day to stop the steal.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.260
And those two lies,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.060
which could have easily just faded away into nothing,

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.580
were continually amplified, legitimized,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.410
and propagated by Donald Trump.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.720
And it resulted in the deaths of five Americans,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.420
the defiling of the seat of our democracy,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.420
and frankly sent a message that we absolutely—

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:24.067
it sent a message that our first,

00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:27.220
our Section One

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.950
powers can be undermined by the Executive.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.940
So the third district of Michigan

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.610
is becoming the impeachment seat, right?

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.930
Because Justin Amash, who you replaced

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.960
when he chose not to run for reelection,

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.220
had voted to impeach Trump a couple of years ago

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.820
or whatever.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.100
You like him, are taking a huge amount

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.330
of guff from constituents.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.550
You mentioned some town halls and stuff, but so far

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.810
it's been announced one of your primary contenders

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.850
from this past election cycle said he's gonna primary you.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.560
You may be censored by the state GOP.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.870
This has happened to other republican congressmen who voted

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.940
to impeach members of Congress.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.750
How do you feel about that?

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.580
You know, and I guess—

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.850
sorry to step in on my own question—

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.490
first, are you elected, like is your philosophy

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.000
that you're elected to reflect your conscience or that—

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.240
or the will of your constituents?

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.960
And then how are you gonna deal with it

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.590
if your own state party says, hey, you're

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.460
you're in big trouble buster?

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.990
Yeah, so my philosophy, to answer that question, is

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:36.320
that you're elected to exercise your judgment

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.867
and you're elected based off of the principles,

00:04:39.867 --> 00:04:43.120
and the values that you espoused during the campaign.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.580
But then ultimately, the job of a representative

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:51.030
is not just to represent the opinion of the mass

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.660
in a point in time, right? Or else what's the point?

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.970
I mean just take a poll, figure out where people are

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.770
and then have that be the direction.

